   "source": [
    "##  Regression Metrics\n",
    "\n",
    "1. MAE (Mean Absolute Error)\n",
    "2. MSE (Mean Squared Error)\n",
    "3. RMSE (Root Mean Squared Error)\n",
    "4. R2 Score (Coeffiecient of Determination)\n",
    "5. Adjusted R2 Score\n",
    "\n",
    "### 1.MAE\n",
    "\n",
    "> MAE=( sum (i=1 to i=n) |yi-yi^| )/n\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Advantages of MAE:\n",
    "\n",
    "- gives error in same unit\n",
    "- robust to outliers\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Disadvantages of MAE:\n",
    "\n",
    "- uses absolute values , and due to this its not differentiable at zero so we can not apply any optimization techniques .\n",
    "\n",
    "### 2. MSE\n",
    "\n",
    ">MSE=( sum (i=1 to i=n) (yi-yi^)^2 )/n\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Advantages of MSE\n",
    "\n",
    "- can be used as loss function as its differentiable.\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Disadvantages of MSE\n",
    "\n",
    "- not robust to outliers.\n",
    "- it gives error in squared units (unit)^2 so we need to find square root of the error to get the actual insights.\n",
    "\n",
    "### 3. RMSE \n",
    "\n",
    ">RMSE=sqrt(MSE)\n",
    ">RMSE=sqrt{ ( sum (i=1 to i=n) (yi-yi^)^2 )/n }\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Advantages of RMSE\n",
    "\n",
    "- the output is in same unit as y coordinate unit.\n",
    "- widely used in Deep Learning\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Disadvantages of RMSE\n",
    "\n",
    "- not that robust to outliers.\n",
    "\n",
    "### 4. R2 Score\n",
    "\n",
    "- also called as Coeffiecient of Determination or Goodness of fit.\n",
    "- we calculate how much our linear regression line is better than the mean line.\n",
    "- R2=1-(SSR)/(SSM)  > where SSR: Sum of Squared Error for Regression Line\n",
    "\n",
    "                       > SSM: Sum of Squared Error for Mean Line\n",
    "  > R2=1-{ (sum i=1 to n (yi-yi^)^2)REG/(sum i=1 to n (yi-yi^)^2)Mean }\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Problem with R2 Score\n",
    "\n",
    "- the R2 score increases as we add newer input columns or dimensions but if we add irrelevant columns to it so ideally it should decrease the R2 score but instead it increases or stays constant.\n",
    "- to fix this we use \"Adjusted R2 Score\n",
    "\n",
    "### Adjusted R2 Score:\n",
    "\n",
    "R2adj=1-[(1-R2)(n-1)/(n-1-k)] where R2=R2 Score,n=number of rows, k= number of independent columns"
   ]
  },
